# Copy to `.env` for local work — and only if you want something here. It is
# no longer a setup step: `bun dev` and `bun run build` both work on a fresh
# clone with no `.env` at all.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS NOT. It is not where the deployed site's settings live.
# `.env` is loaded in every mode, so anything in it reaches `vite build` too —
# and a build is what gets uploaded. That is exactly how this file once became
# a hazard: it told you to point the app at localhost, and a hand-run
# `bun run deploy` would then publish a live site calling your own machine.
# The production values are in `apps/editor/.env.production`, which is
# committed and which Vite ranks ABOVE this file in production mode, so
# nothing you write here can reach a deployed bundle. Put production settings
# there, local ones here.
#
# Every variable is validated by `src/env.schema.ts`, in Node before anything
# is emitted, so a bad value fails the build rather than reaching a visitor.

# OPTIONAL now, and only for development. `.env.production` states the real
# API, and `src/env.schema.ts` defaults this to localhost for `bun dev`, so the
# line below is only worth uncommenting to point dev at a worker on some other
# port. It has no effect on `vite build`.
#   local      → http://localhost:8787   (what `bun dev` already defaults to)
#   production → declared in .env.production, not here
# VITE_API_URL=http://localhost:8787

# OPTIONAL. Error reporting. Absent means Sentry is never initialised and
# `reportIssue` stays silent — the build still succeeds, on purpose.
# VITE_SENTRY_DSN=

# OPTIONAL, build-time only, never in the bundle. Set all three in CI to
# upload source maps; the build then deletes them so they are never served
# from `dist/`. Absent means no source maps are generated at all.
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=
# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=

# OPTIONAL. Product analytics. Absent means the PostHog SDK — a separate
# ~75KB gzip chunk — is never downloaded by anyone, so leaving this unset
# costs nothing rather than shipping dead weight.
# VITE_POSTHOG_KEY=
# Region-specific, and this project is on US — which is also the schema's
# default, so this line stays commented and copying it uncommented is still
# correct. An EU project would use https://eu.i.posthog.com instead.
# VITE_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com
